I did not use Maven/Grail/Ivy. did I wrong something?
here is my code:

package renjin;

import javax.script.ScriptEngine;
import javax.script.ScriptException;
import org.renjin.script.RenjinScriptEngineFactory;

/**
 *
 * @author Aldi
 */
public class Renjin {

    /**
     * @param args the command line arguments
     */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws ScriptException {
        // TODO code application logic here
        RenjinScriptEngineFactory factory = new RenjinScriptEngineFactory();
        // create a Renjin engine:
        ScriptEngine engine = factory.getScriptEngine();

        engine.eval("RealData <- read.csv('E:/R Project/Original Data Cleaning Data Historis.csv', header = TRUE)");
        engine.eval("print(RealData)");
        engine.eval("library(e1071)");
        engine.eval("tune.out <- tune(svm, hasil~., data=RealData, kernel=\"radial\",ranges = list(cost = c(1:10),gamma = c (1:4)))");
        engine.eval("summary(tune.out)");
        engine.eval("svmfit <- svm(hasil~., data=RealData, kernel=\"radial\", cost=2, gamma=4)");
        engine.eval("summary(svmfit)");

    }
}

Pada Senin, 07 Agustus 2017 21.32.59 UTC+7, Alexander Bertram menulis:
> It looks like the R package "class" is not on your classpath.
>
> "class" is a transitive dependency of e1071, so if you are using Maven/Grail/Ivy, this should be automatically added to your classpath.
>
> See the documentation on project setup here:
> http://docs.renjin.org/en/latest/library/project-setup.html
>
> Also make sure you are using the latest version of Renjin, as I recall that there was once an issue with packages with names like "class" that are reserved in the Java language.
>
> Best,
> Alex
